  "query_preview": "..\nA tropical low has moved inland into Mexico SW of Hermosillo \nwith an upper level low west of central Baja. We are also seeing\nmultiple embedded MCVs exiting Graham/Greenlee.\n\nThese features are setting the stage for a rather robust and\npotentially significant day across Arizona. Latest CAMs have\nvarying solutions, but they all have some flavor of a broad\nareal expansion of current showers/storms with intensification \nthrough the rest of the afternoon. While we are seeing some \ndeeper level convection, much of this is fairly shallow as of \nnow. However these are very efficient rainfall producers due to \nthe highly anomalous PWATS and favorable tall skinny CAPE \nsoundings supporting a tropical regime. This environment, in \nconjunction with deep warm cloud rain processes, is providing 1\"\nto nearly 1.5\" rainfall rates in 15-30 mins. Would expect this \nto continue and maximize as deeper convection begins to develop \naround early afternoon. In fact, the low level circulation that\nhas moved inland into Mexico is showing renewed signs of \nrapidly cooling cloud tops on satellite.\n\nAs this low pressure moves into southern AZ this afternoon, \nexpect coverage to increase with an increasing flash flood risk.\nGiven what we have seen this morning, high PWATS, saturated \ngrounds, and the Moderate Risk ERO, we could see some isolated \ninstances of significant flash flooding. Deciphering on exactly \nwhere that could be is tricky, but there is some indication it \nwill follow the low pressure circulation and just north, NE and \neast of the low. That would place portions of Santa Cruz, \ncentral and eastern Pima, and Pinal COs in the track. This is \nlining up well with the initial runs of the WoFS showing a high\nprobability of seeing 2\" of rain west of Tucson in the next few\nhrs. 90th percentile is showing widespread 1-2\" with isolated \nto scattered areas of 2-3\". With what has already fallen, there\ncould even be some localized 4\"+ rainfall amounts. However these\nwould be isolated in nature and be dependent on where they fall.  \n\nThere could also be a trailing band of convection, which could \ntrain over some areas this evening. This is supported by 12Z \nHREF now showing a 30% contour of greater than 3 inches of rain \nin 3 hrs. This signal is just west of the Tucson Metro aligned \nwell with radar trends and WoFS runs, but the 10% contours is \ncovering most of the CWA from Tucson west and north into central\nAZ. \n\nWhile the severe risk is low, we cannot rule out a wet\nmicroburst or two with a few isolated gusts approaching 60 mph.\nOtherwise, very heavy rain and flash flooding is the main \nthreat. \n\nThursday: Precipitable water remains elevated for this time of \nyear with a slight risk for excessive rainfall continuing east \nand south of Tucson. Showers and storms that develop will still \nhave the potential for locally heavy rainfall, but without the \ndisturbance nearby there will be a lack of forcing to help aid \nin higher rainfall rates like Wednesday.\n\nFriday through Tuesday: Drier air finally begins to filter into\nsoutheast Arizona as flow aloft shifts more \nwesterly/southwesterly. This could help limit thunderstorm \nactivity somewhat and keep activity mainly east and south of \nTucson. High temperatures return to at or slightly above normal.\n\n8 to 14 day outlooks (Wed Sep 23 to Tue Sep 29)\nTemperature: Leaning above normal, 39-42% chance\nPrecipitation: Leaning above normal, 39-42% chance",
